The palindrome check and cut update can be done together. I keep `cuts[i]` as the best cut count for the first `i` characters. A sentinel value at `cuts[0]` makes a palindrome starting at index zero easy to handle.

For each character, the code expands both odd-length and even-length palindromes. During a dry run on `"aab"`, the center between the two `a` characters finds `"aa"`, so the value for the prefix `"aa"` becomes one piece. The final `"b"` gives two pieces total, or one cut.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int minCut(string s) {
        int n = static_cast<int>(s.size());
        if (n == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        vector<int> cuts(n + 1, INT_MAX);
        cuts[0] = -1;

        for (int center = 0; center < n; center++) {
            for (int left = center, right = center;
                 left >= 0 && right < n && s[left] == s[right];
                 left--, right++) {
                cuts[right + 1] = min(cuts[right + 1], cuts[left] + 1);
            }

            for (int left = center, right = center + 1;
                 left >= 0 && right < n && s[left] == s[right];
                 left--, right++) {
                cuts[right + 1] = min(cuts[right + 1], cuts[left] + 1);
            }
        }

        return cuts[n];
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(n)`. The input string is only read, not changed.

This version starts at a position and tries every palindrome that begins there. `best(start)` means the minimum number of pieces needed for `s[start:]`. The answer is the number of pieces minus one.

Both the palindrome test and the suffix result are cached. That prevents the same substring checks from being repeated many times. The trade-off is that the two caches together use quadratic space.

```python
class Solution:
    def minCut(self, s):
        n = len(s)
        if n <= 1:
            return 0

        palindrome_cache = {}
        result_cache = {}

        def is_palindrome(left, right):
            if left >= right:
                return True

            key = (left, right)
            if key in palindrome_cache:
                return palindrome_cache[key]

            if s[left] != s[right]:
                palindrome_cache[key] = False
            else:
                palindrome_cache[key] = is_palindrome(left + 1, right - 1)

            return palindrome_cache[key]

        def best(start):
            if start == n:
                return 0
            if start in result_cache:
                return result_cache[start]

            pieces = n - start
            for end in range(start, n):
                if is_palindrome(start, end):
                    pieces = min(pieces, 1 + best(end + 1))

            result_cache[start] = pieces
            return pieces

        return best(0) - 1
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because each start position tries the possible ending positions, with palindrome results cached.
Space complexity: `O(n^2)` for the palindrome cache, plus `O(n)` for the suffix cache and recursion stack. A string made of one repeated character also works because the cache avoids repeated work.

I used two DP parts here. First, `pal[start][end]` records whether a substring is a palindrome. Then `cuts[i]` stores the minimum number of palindrome pieces in the first `i` characters.

For `"aab"`, the useful palindrome pieces are `"a"`, `"aa"`, and `"b"`. The best split is `"aa" | "b"`, which has two pieces and therefore one cut.

```java
class Solution {
    int minCut(String s) {
        int n = s.length();
        if (n <= 1) {
            return 0;
        }

        boolean[][] pal = new boolean[n][n];

        for (int start = n - 1; start >= 0; start--) {
            for (int end = start; end < n; end++) {
                if (s.charAt(start) == s.charAt(end)
                        && (end - start <= 2 || pal[start + 1][end - 1])) {
                    pal[start][end] = true;
                }
            }
        }

        int[] cuts = new int[n + 1];
        cuts[0] = 0;

        for (int end = 1; end <= n; end++) {
            cuts[end] = end;

            for (int start = 0; start < end; start++) {
                if (pal[start][end - 1]) {
                    cuts[end] = Math.min(cuts[end], cuts[start] + 1);
                }
            }
        }

        return cuts[n] - 1;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(n^2)` for the palindrome table. An empty or one-character string returns zero directly.

I viewed every position as a point between characters. If `s[left:right + 1]` is a palindrome, there is a jump from `left` to `right + 1`. A valid partition is then a path from position `0` to position `n`.

The BFS finds the fewest palindrome pieces. Subtracting one changes the number of pieces into the number of cuts. For a quick dry run with `"aab"`, the jumps include `0 -> 1`, `0 -> 2`, and `2 -> 3`. The shortest path is `0 -> 2 -> 3`, so the result is `2 - 1 = 1`.

```python
from collections import deque

class Solution:
    def minCut(self, s):
        n = len(s)
        if n <= 1:
            return 0

        pal = [[False] * n for _ in range(n)]
        for left in range(n - 1, -1, -1):
            for right in range(left, n):
                if s[left] == s[right] and (
                    right - left <= 2 or pal[left + 1][right - 1]
                ):
                    pal[left][right] = True

        distance = [-1] * (n + 1)
        distance[0] = 0
        queue = deque([0])

        while queue:
            left = queue.popleft()

            if left == n:
                break

            for right in range(left, n):
                if pal[left][right]:
                    next_position = right + 1
                    if distance[next_position] == -1:
                        distance[next_position] = distance[left] + 1
                        queue.append(next_position)

        return distance[n] - 1
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`, including the palindrome table and all possible jumps.
Space complexity: `O(n^2)` for the table, with `O(n)` more space for BFS. The algorithm does not modify the input string.

Here the DP state is an interval instead of only a prefix. `dp[left][right]` stores the minimum cuts for the substring from `left` to `right`.

The palindrome table is filled first. If the whole interval is a palindrome, its cut count is zero. Otherwise, every split point is tested. This is a more direct version to understand, but it checks many intervals and split points, so it is slower than the one-dimensional versions.

```java
class Solution {
    int minCut(String s) {
        int n = s.length();
        if (n <= 1) {
            return 0;
        }

        boolean[][] pal = new boolean[n][n];
        for (int length = 1; length <= n; length++) {
            for (int left = 0; left + length <= n; left++) {
                int right = left + length - 1;
                if (s.charAt(left) == s.charAt(right)
                        && (length <= 2 || pal[left + 1][right - 1])) {
                    pal[left][right] = true;
                }
            }
        }

        int[][] dp = new int[n][n];

        for (int length = 2; length <= n; length++) {
            for (int left = 0; left + length <= n; left++) {
                int right = left + length - 1;

                if (pal[left][right]) {
                    dp[left][right] = 0;
                    continue;
                }

                int best = length - 1;
                for (int split = left; split < right; split++) {
                    best = Math.min(best,
                            dp[left][split] + dp[split + 1][right] + 1);
                }
                dp[left][right] = best;
            }
        }

        return dp[0][n - 1];
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^3)`, from trying every interval and split point.
Space complexity: `O(n^2)` for the two-dimensional arrays. Strings of length zero or one need no split, so they return zero.
